<title>GERMANY: Siemens signs DM2 bln Bangkok transit deal.</title>
<headline>Siemens signs DM2 bln Bangkok transit deal.</headline>
<dateline>BONN 1996-10-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>Siemens AG said on Monday that it, along with Adtranz (Great Britain) and Balfour Beatty, had signed a two billion mark contract to supply equipment for an elevated road and transit system in Bangkok, capital of Thailand.</p>
<p>Adtranz (Great Britain) is the UK unit of the ABB/Daimler Benz rail joint venture. Balfour Beatty is a unit of BICC Plc.</p>
<p>The contract, signed in the German transport ministry, was also inked by the system's concession holder, Gordon Wu of Hong Kong developers Hopewell Holding Ltd.</p>
<p>The Siemens share of the contract is worth 1.3 billion marks, while the two British partners will share the remaining 700 million marks. The project should be ready for handover in October 1998, Siemens said in a statement.</p>
<p>Hopewell signed a 38-year concession rights deal with the State Railway of Thailand in 1990 to build a transit system capable of moving 100,000 passengers per hour when completed.</p>
<p>-- Bonn newsroom, 49-228-26097150</p>
